## Summary

PowerShell console flashing is not fully resolved by the Windows background-process fixes. Please investigate the remaining persistent PowerShell parser launch path rather than treating all console flashing as resolved by 0.159.2.

Issue #44768 was closed on September 30 with a statement that 0.159.2 should fix this. Before that closure, we posted a reproducible remaining failure with a passing minimal fix: https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/44768#issuecomment-5901816514.

## Versions and evidence boundaries

- Windows 11 build 26200, Windows PowerShell (`powershell.exe`).
- User-observed terminal/PowerShell flashes occurred during ordinary prompts and tool calls with the official 0.159.0 daemon. Our custom patched 0.159.0 daemon eliminated the observed flashes; follow-up tool calls and window tracing confirmed that result.
- The isolated parser regression below was reproduced against upstream main `bd5185820d`, after the broader console fixes had landed.
- On September 30, we inspected the newest published alpha, **0.161.0-alpha.5**. Its persistent parser still uses plain `std::process::Command::new` followed by `command.spawn()`, without `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` or the shared background-command helper: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.161.0-alpha.5/codex-rs/shell-command/src/command_safety/powershell_parser.rs#L115-L129.
- We have **not** installed or runtime-tested the official 0.159.2 or 0.161.0-alpha.5 binary. The alpha finding is a source inspection, and the runtime evidence is the upstream-source regression below. We are not claiming a new full-binary reproduction on those releases.

## Expected behavior

A noninteractive parser helper started by a detached/background daemon must not allocate a console window. Normal interactive ConPTY behavior should remain intact.

## Other reports

These are additional reports of visible Windows console/PowerShell flashes, not proof that every report has the same underlying cause or was tested on the latest release:

- #48074 — Windows: terminal windows repeatedly flash during requests after installing the Codex daemon (open).
- #49371 — Windows native CLI: visible CMD/PowerShell windows flash for every shell tool call (open).
- #49264 — Windows: CLI flashes a Windows Terminal window for every spawned command (regression, app-server daemon) (open).
- #36560 — Codex Windows PowerShell Console Flash (open).

## Requested follow-up

Please cover `PowershellParserProcess::spawn` with Windows console suppression and a detached-parent regression test, and validate ordinary prompt/tool execution on Windows with the managed daemon. The broader fixes in #49164 and #49308 are useful, but do not modify this independent parser spawn site. Please keep this remaining path tracked until it is addressed.

## Detailed reproduction, minimal fix, and full regression

### Remaining console window: persistent PowerShell parser

Thanks for #49164 and #49308. After comparing a locally verified Windows console-suppression patch against current `main`, the persistent PowerShell parser is the one remaining launch path from that patch that is still missing suppression.

**Verified base:** `bd5185820d` (current `main` when tested), Windows 11 build 26200, using Windows PowerShell (`powershell.exe`) as selected by the existing test helper.

In [`PowershellParserProcess::spawn`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/bd5185820d/codex-rs/shell-command/src/command_safety/powershell_parser.rs#L115-L129), the code constructs a plain `std::process::Command` and calls `spawn()` without `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`. It does not use the new background-command or Job Object helpers.

### Reproduction and results

I added the regression below to the existing Windows-only tests module in that file. It starts an isolated test process with `DETACHED_PROCESS`, launches the actual persistent parser, verifies that parsing `Get-Location` works, and checks the parser console window from the isolated process.

```powershell
just test --release -p codex-shell-command --lib -E 'test(parser_process_has_no_console_window_from_detached_parent)'
```

On the unchanged production code, the test failed on both nextest attempts with:

```text
PowerShell parser created a console window
```

Adding a Windows-only `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` flag immediately before `command.spawn()` made the same regression pass. The full affected unit suite then passed:

```text
just test --release -p codex-shell-command --lib
174 tests run: 174 passed, 0 skipped
```

The minimal local fix is:

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
{
    use std::os::windows::process::CommandExt;
    const CREATE_NO_WINDOW: u32 = 0x0800_0000;
    command.creation_flags(CREATE_NO_WINDOW);
}
```

Reusing the new shared `background_command` helper would also fit the upstream implementation if preferred. This parser is a background helper with piped stdin/stdout; interactive ConPTY creation is separate.

<details>
<summary>Detached-parent regression added to the existing Windows test module</summary>

```rust
    #[test]
    fn parser_process_has_no_console_window_from_detached_parent() {
        use std::os::windows::process::CommandExt;

        const CHILD_MARKER: &str = "CODEX_POWERSHELL_PARSER_CONSOLE_TEST";
        if std::env::var_os(CHILD_MARKER).is_none() {
            let output = Command::new(std::env::current_exe().unwrap())
                .args([
                    "parser_process_has_no_console_window_from_detached_parent",
                    "--test-threads=1",
                    "--nocapture",
                ])
                .env(CHILD_MARKER, "1")
                .creation_flags(0x00000008)
                .output()
                .unwrap();
            assert!(
                output.status.success(),
                "detached parser test failed: {}{}",
                String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout),
                String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stderr),
            );
            return;
        }

        let powershell = try_find_powershell_executable_blocking()
            .expect("PowerShell is required for the detached parser regression");
        let mut parser =
            PowershellParserProcess::spawn(powershell.as_path().to_str().unwrap()).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(
            parser.parse("Get-Location").unwrap(),
            PowershellParseOutcome::Commands(vec![vec!["Get-Location".to_string()]]),
        );

        #[link(name = "kernel32")]
        unsafe extern "system" {
            fn AttachConsole(process_id: u32) -> i32;
            fn FreeConsole() -> i32;
            fn GetConsoleWindow() -> *mut std::ffi::c_void;
        }

        // CREATE_NO_WINDOW may still provide an attachable headless console.
        // Inspect its window from this isolated process after the parser responds.
        let attached = unsafe { AttachConsole(parser.child.id()) };
        if attached == 0 {
            assert_eq!(std::io::Error::last_os_error().raw_os_error(), Some(6));
        } else {
            let window = unsafe { GetConsoleWindow() };
            unsafe { FreeConsole() };
            assert!(
                window.is_null(),
                "PowerShell parser created a console window"
            );
        }
    }
```

</details>

The test intentionally checks `GetConsoleWindow()` rather than requiring `AttachConsole()` to fail: a headless PowerShell process can still expose an attachable console with no window.

This is a focused reproduction on the source commit above, not a full runtime validation of the official 0.159.2 binary. Sharing it here in accordance with the contribution guide's request for issue reports and root-cause analysis instead of external PRs. Related: #48074.

### What variant of Codex are you using?

The `codex-utils-pty` Rust crate, used as a library dependency at `rust-v0.154.0`.

### What feature would you like to see?

An opt-in, bounded way to look at an exited PTY child before `codex-utils-pty` reaps it.

Today both Unix PTY spawn paths (`spawn_process_portable` and `spawn_process_preserving_fds` in `utils/pty/src/pty.rs`) reap the child in their own waiter as soon as it exits. That is the right default, and the waiter must stay the only reaper. But a caller that accounts for a launch by its process group cannot inspect the group while the exited root still holds its PID and process group ID. Once the root is reaped, a descendant that ignored SIGHUP and stayed in the group can no longer be attributed with certainty, because the ID may be reused. The caller cannot reap the child itself, and a second waiter would race the existing one.

Proposed shape (implemented in the reference patch below):

- `spawn_pty_process_observed(program, args, cwd, env, arg0, size, inherited_fds, observer)`, the same as `spawn_pty_process` plus an `observe::ExitObserver { opportunity: oneshot::Sender<ExitOpportunity>, bound: Duration, outcome: oneshot::Sender<ObservationOutcome> }`. The observer is registered before the spawn, so even a very fast exit cannot be missed.
- When the child exits, the existing waiter calls `waitid(P_PID, pid, WEXITED | WNOWAIT)`. The child stays a zombie and keeps its PID and process group ID.
- The waiter then publishes one `ExitOpportunity`, which carries no PID, and waits until it is acknowledged or the bound passes. Only then does it reap through the original child object, exactly as today.
- After the reap, and before the exit code is sent, it reports one of:
  - `Completed`;
  - `TimedOut`;
  - `Failed`, when the observer dropped the opportunity or stopped listening;
  - `Unavailable`, when the exit could not be seen without reaping.

  An `acknowledge()` after the bound returns `OpportunityExpired`.
- Without an observer, nothing changes: `spawn_pty_process` and every existing path are untouched. Platforms other than Unix refuse an observed spawn before creating a process.

The bound keeps every failure finite. A stalled observer delays the reap by at most the bound, and a dropped one ends the wait at once. Termination and handle Drop during the window still reach the process group, which the unreaped child keeps, and the child is reaped exactly once.

### Additional information

- Reference implementation: a patch to `rust-v0.154.0`, one commit exported with `git format-patch`: https://github.com/novelKR/CodeSpace/blob/374a3fc6f9897ce97d1eb9b442df8d0f627aba08/third_party/codex-patches/0001-utils-pty-pre-reap-observation.patch. It is 304 added and 5 removed lines of product code (`observe.rs`, `pty.rs`, `lib.rs`) plus nine tests, each run on both Unix paths. The tests cover:
  - the exit seen before the reap;
  - the bound, including a late acknowledgement refused;
  - a dropped opportunity and a dropped observer;
  - a SIGHUP-ignoring descendant still in the root's process group during the window;
  - termination in the window;
  - a handle dropped in the window;
  - 256 KiB of output draining fully;
  - a runtime shutdown held only for the bound.
- Verified on Linux x86_64 (36 crate tests) and macOS (38; two existing tests are macOS-only): every test passes in each of 30 repeated runs, and clippy is clean on Linux, macOS and Windows targets. On macOS we also ran it under our resource manager: a surviving descendant stayed attributed to its launch until it ended (10/10), a stalled observer was reaped at its 500 ms bound, and the observer added about 4 ms at the median to the exit report.
- `ProcessDriver` / `spawn_from_driver` would make the caller own PTY allocation, terminal setup, I/O and reaping, duplicating this crate. A hook inside the existing single waiter avoids that.
- CodeSpace has used it for its governed PTY executions since 2026-10-12 (https://github.com/novelKR/CodeSpace/pull/95): the resource manager observes the exited root's process group in the window, so a descendant that outlives its root stays attributed to its launch until it ends.
- We carry this as a downstream patch and will drop it once an upstream equivalent exists. Any API shape that keeps the single waiter, a bound and an outcome would meet our need.

## Summary
When `codex` (or a `codex` test binary / child CLI sub-process) runs inside a `workspace-write` or `read-only` sandbox where `CODEX_HOME` (`~/.codex`) is outside the writable roots, `arg0_dispatch()` attempts to create a new temporary directory under `~/.codex/tmp/arg0` and call `chmod(0o700)` on `~/.codex/tmp/arg0`. This fails with `EPERM`/`EACCES` (`PermissionDenied`) or `EROFS` (`ReadOnlyFilesystem`), printing:

```text
WARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Operation not permitted (os error 1)
```

As a result, `Arg0PathEntryGuard` is `None`, and `main_execve_wrapper_exe` / `codex_linux_sandbox_exe` are dropped even when a parent `codex` process has already created and locked a valid `codex-arg0*` alias directory under `~/.codex/tmp/arg0` and passed it via `PATH`.

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Start a parent `codex` session (which creates `~/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg0...` and prepends it to `PATH`).
2. Inside a `workspace-write` sandbox where `~/.codex` is read-only, invoke a child `codex` sub-process (for example `codex --version` or a `codex-core` test).
3. Observe `WARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Operation not permitted (os error 1)` on stderr and `Arg0PathEntryGuard = None`.

## Expected Behavior
When creating a new per-session directory under `CODEX_HOME/tmp/arg0` fails with `PermissionDenied` or `ReadOnlyFilesystem`, `codex-arg0` should inspect `existing_path` (`PATH`) for an already-provisioned `codex-arg0*` directory under `CODEX_HOME/tmp/arg0` whose helper aliases (`apply_patch`, `applypatch`, `codex-execve-wrapper`, and `codex-linux-sandbox` on Linux) resolve to the current executable, and reuse those `Arg0DispatchPaths` without owning or deleting the parent's `TempDir` or `.lock` file.

## Environment
- Codex CLI version: `0.162.1` (`aarch64-apple-darwin`)
- Codex Desktop version: `26.1007.71326` (embedded CLI `0.162.0-alpha.17.2`)
- OS: macOS `aarch64`

## Root-Cause Analysis & Verification

## Summary
- Extracts `prepare_path_entry_for_codex_aliases_in(codex_home, existing_path)` and `create_path_entry_in_temp_root(...)` in `codex-rs/arg0/src/lib.rs`.
- Creates `temp_dir` via `tempdir_in(temp_root)` and acquires its `.lock` before running `set_permissions(0o700)` and `janitor_cleanup(temp_root)`, avoiding permission mutation or janitor warning noise when `temp_root` is read-only.
- Adds `try_reuse_existing_path_entry(temp_root, existing_path)` and `Arg0PathEntryGuard::reused(paths)` so child `codex` invocations running inside sandboxes where `CODEX_HOME` is read-only (`PermissionDenied` / `ReadOnlyFilesystem`) reuse a verified inherited `codex-arg0*` entry from `PATH` whose helper symlinks resolve to `current_exe()`.

## Verification
- `cargo test --offline -p codex-arg0` (8 passed, including `prepare_path_entry_reuses_inherited_alias_dir_when_temp_root_is_read_only`)
- `cargo clippy --offline -p codex-arg0 --all-targets --no-deps -- -D warnings` (0 warnings)

